μ = 19.625, σ = 15.305. With |z| > 2: **60** is an outlier (z = 2.638). With the stricter |z| > 3, nothing is flagged: one big value inflates σ enough to hide itself.

<!-- #endregion d3 -->
<!-- #region d4 -->

| x | y | x − x̄ | y − ȳ | product | (x − x̄)² | (y − ȳ)² |
|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 10 | 40 | −20 | 11 | −220 | 400 | 121 |
| 20 | 35 | −10 | 6 | −60 | 100 | 36 |
| 30 | 30 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| 40 | 22 | 10 | −7 | −70 | 100 | 49 |
| 50 | 18 | 20 | −11 | −220 | 400 | 121 |
|  |  |  | **Σ** | **−570** | **1000** | **328** |

x̄ = 30, ȳ = 29. r = −570/√(1000 × 328) = **−0.9953**: a very strong **negative** linear relationship.

<!-- #endregion d4 -->
<!-- #region d5 -->

Σx = 590, Σy = 6680, Σx² = 71900, Σxy = 815400, n = 5.

$$ \begin{aligned} a &= \frac{5(815400) - (590)(6680)}{5(71900) - (590)^2} = \frac{135800}{11400} = 11.9123 \\ b &= \bar y - a\bar x = 1336 - 11.9123 \times 118 = -69.65 \end{aligned} $$

**ŷ = 11.9123x − 69.65.** For an advertisement spend of 200 the predicted sales are 11.9123 × 200 − 69.65 = **2312.81**. R² = 1 − 26382.5/349920 = 0.925. (200 is outside the data, 90–150, so this is an extrapolation.)

<!-- #endregion d5 -->
<!-- #region d6 -->

f(w) = w² − 4w + 5, so f′(w) = 2w − 4; the minimum is at w = 2.

| Iteration | w (old) | f′(w) | new w |
|:-:|--:|--:|---|
| 1 | 0 | −4 | 0 − 0.25 × (−4) = **1** |
| 2 | 1 | −2 | 1 − 0.25 × (−2) = **1.5** |
| 3 | 1.5 | −1 | 1.5 − 0.25 × (−1) = **1.75** |

Each step halves the gap to 2 (2 → 1 → 0.5 → 0.25 away).

<!-- #endregion d6 -->
<!-- #region d7 -->

| x | y | ŷ | e = ŷ − y | e·x |
|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|
| 2 | 5 | 0 | −5 | −10 |
| 4 | 9 | 0 | −9 | −36 |
| 6 | 13 | 0 | −13 | −78 |
|  |  | **Σ** | **−27** | **−124** |

$$ \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial J}{\partial w} &= \tfrac{1}{n}\textstyle\sum e\,x = \tfrac{1}{3}(-124) = -41.3333, & w &= 0 - 0.01(-41.3333) = \mathbf{0.4133} \\ \frac{\partial J}{\partial b} &= \tfrac{1}{n}\textstyle\sum e = \tfrac{1}{3}(-27) = -9, & b &= 0 - 0.01(-9) = \mathbf{0.09} \end{aligned} $$

With J = (1/2n)Σ(ŷ − y)² the 2 cancels, so the gradient has 1/n in front. With the 2/n convention every gradient, and so every step, doubles.

<!-- #endregion d7 -->
<!-- #region d8 -->

| x₁ | x₂ | y | ŷ | e | e·x₁ | e·x₂ |
|:-:|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 1 | 1 | 4 | 0 | −4 | −4 | −4 |
| 2 | 0 | 5 | 0 | −5 | −10 | 0 |
| 0 | 2 | 6 | 0 | −6 | 0 | −12 |
|  |  |  | **Σ** | **−15** | **−14** | **−16** |

Gradients (2/n convention): ∂J/∂θ₀ = (2/3)(−15) = −10, ∂J/∂θ₁ = (2/3)(−14) = −9.3333, ∂J/∂θ₂ = (2/3)(−16) = −10.6667.

**After one iteration: θ₀ = 1, θ₁ = 0.9333, θ₂ = 1.0667.**

<!-- #endregion d8 -->
<!-- #region d9 -->

z = −4 + 0.05 × 60 = −1; p = 1/(1 + e^1) = 1/(1 + 2.7183) = **0.2689** → predicted class **0** (fail) at threshold 0.5.

Odds = p/(1 − p) = 0.3679 (= e^z = e^−1). Odds ratio per extra hour = e^0.05 = **1.0513**: each hour multiplies the odds of passing by about 1.05 (+5.13%). p = 0.5 exactly when z = 0, that is −4 + 0.05x = 0, so **x = 80 hours**.

